title a line is named by a pair -- the weave merges two branches
prompt the baton; my lane names the identity gap as the next real work

think The card's Patchouli account named the crux in one sentence: the identity gap stays unblocked, and closing it wants a fourth field on Line. That is Lindy-first (mantra is the version store 120 files reach into) and crux-first (the hard, still-tractable move that opens the rest), so I took it rather than opening anything new.
think Read the module before touching it. pos is a u32 from a per-weave counter next_pos, so two weaves that parted hand the same small integer to different lines, union_into joins on pos alone, sees one position with two texts, and returns PositionTextDisagrees. The merge law was sound; the identity was too narrow. Claim 6 of the merge witness existed to PROVE that gap.
think Chose the shape by asking where a site belongs. A site is the hand making an edit; a weave is the history several hands merge into one. Holding site on Weave would make it a field merge has to reconcile, and there is no honest answer to whose a merged history is -- so it rides on Diff, empty() keeps its signature, and merge stays commutative field for field.
think Chose the pair over a fused Pos struct because pos and site do two different jobs -- where in the document, and whose counter -- and the ordering key wants pos first. LineId names the pair for the outside world, with eq and less_than written ONCE, since an order spelled at each call site is an order those sites may come to disagree about, which for a merge means two sides computing two documents from one history.
think Kept PositionTextDisagrees rather than renaming it. The pair IS the position now; what was wrong was the width rather than the word. Renaming would have spent the lap on seven files of spelling. Named the choice in the head so a later reader sees it was chosen rather than missed.
think My first postcondition assert was true by construction and proved nothing, so I replaced it with the invariant the pair actually buys: a fresh insert's identity is one this weave does not already hold, asserted at construction rather than trusted.
think Turned claim 6 from a refusal into its opposite and added claim 9 in both witnesses, because the two are one sentence read from both sides -- two branches merge, one site issuing one name twice refuses -- and neither reads right alone. Claim 9 is built by hand since apply cannot produce that state.
think tame_style_check caught assert(saw_x and saw_z) as a compound assert. Split it, and the split is better: a failure now says WHICH side was dropped.
think Then the diff control read break_not_caught. My type change turned its elder-form plant into a no-op: the sed matched nothing, the pen was byte-identical, and the phase built the UNMUTATED module and read 0. That is a red rather than a chore -- a plant is a claim about a file and nothing was checking that claim.
think Measured before booking it rather than after. 102 of 155 tracked controls mutate a pen with sed; 8 carry a cmp -s, and reading all eight, every one uses it to prove a plant landed -- five lanes, five spellings, five hands who each met this alone. That is a loom rather than a lantern, and 94 controls still have no such check.
think Repaired only my three, per read scope: touch means the lane's own rooms. The 94 want either 94 edits or one shared helper the fixtures import, which is a shape decision rather than a lap, so it is named on the card as Keaton's.
think The card stood at 40,951 bytes of a 40,960 bound before I wrote a word. Trimmed my own account to 1,836 first, then folded Bakery's %510 account -- CLOSED on the ledger, so a finished section -- whole and unedited onto the same shelf, leaving a pointer. Folding accretes where shaving destroys.
think Found one more thing I did not book: the card's Pheromone account cites %506 for a proof two living pages cite, and %506 is MY row about the module head enumeration; that account's subject is %504 in the ledger. Measured the mechanical half -- of 71 distinct numbers the card cites, all but the four custody gates hold a real row -- so existence is clean and only agreement drifted. One firing, in a lane not mine, so I named it rather than editing a peer's account or booking a second row.

obs The site earns its keep twice, which is why one field was enough: it separates two branches' lines AND orders them, so a total order over the pair leaves nothing for a tiebreak to invent.
obs The gap that stays open is the one I did NOT close, and the head says so rather than claiming otherwise: two branches each inserting SEVERAL lines interleave line by line, since pos alone decides and both counted from the same place. An anchor per insert closes it and widens Diff.
obs A control's plants are literal lines of the module they break, so the lap that edits one of those lines disarms its plant silently. The verdict loop caught mine by luck rather than design -- it demands that phase be non-zero, and a plant whose absence exits non-zero for an unrelated reason would have read ok.
obs No cold roster pass opened this lap; the hot full pass at the close is what measures the tree the commit ships. Naming it so the record is accurate rather than implied.
obs THE HOT PASS CAUGHT ME, AND IT WAS %500'S OWN INSTRUMENT. Five reds: rye_struct_fields and mantra_glow_tend_limb1 and limb3, all three mine and all three the same mechanism -- a museum placard in src/shape/ naming Line's field count as three and Diff's as two, held to the code by a guard %500 taught to COUNT rather than grep for presence, precisely because a fourth field used to pass it. The fourth field arrived and it refused on that lap. Both placards moved with their reason written on them, and every pin now holds the ORDER as well as the count, since a reorder is a silent wire change.
obs AND THE FIFTH RED WAS MY OWN TREE'S PARKED RECORDS, WHICH TURNED OUT TO BE MINE. Two complete Patchouli session logs -- 20260906.180937 and 20260906.195019, 44 and 50 lines -- stood in the dead-letter box and on no ref but a pier/ park, and their shelf rows were gone with them. Recovered both at their own paths with their rows restored in stamp order: unlanded 2 -> 0, parked 2 -> 0, verdict ok. What I did NOT recover from the same stash is the parked ITINERARY nib and REDS headline, both superseded by this lap's own; landing them would move a living pin backwards.
obs THE AIR ROW ASKED ME TO PRESS EACH REFLEX AND ONE POST GAVE. TAME Core states aim >=2 asserts per fn, and nothing measures it: tame_style_check reads bans and file-level ratchets, so a zero-assert FUNCTION in a module full of asserts is invisible. My own four new accessors -- LineId.eq, LineId.less_than, Line.id, Note.id -- are exactly that shape, and I judge zero right for a two-line total function over two integers, yet that is a judgment no instrument shares. Not booked: nothing is wrong, and a law with no meter is a different thing from a law broken. Written down because the row that feels says to write down the post the hand passes through.

